-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Retro Remote Debug Controller (RRDC) — Doomsday One fork
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is a fork of Mednafen that adds the Retro Remote Debug Controller: a small
HTTP control surface an external harness can drive to inspect and steer the
running emulator (read memory / CPU registers, grab the displayed framebuffer,
inject input, reset, etc.). It is the same contract implemented by the sibling
x16-emulator and neo6502 forks, so one test harness can drive every emulator in
the toolchain. The shared spec lives at:

	https://github.com/doomsdayonecom/retro-remote-debug-controller

The control backend lives entirely under src/control/ and is dispatched per
running system, so PC Engine and Saturn support reuse the same core once their
backends land. Today the PC-FX backend (src/control/pcfx_control.cpp) is the
reference implementation.


 Building with RRDC
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

RRDC is OPT-IN. A plain build produces a stock, upstream-parity Mednafen:

	./autogen.sh          # only needed from a fresh git checkout
	./configure           # RRDC off by default
	make

To compile the control backend in, pass --enable-rrdc:

	./configure --enable-rrdc
	make

The flag defines WANT_RRDC (guarding the hooks in src/drivers/main.cpp and the
accessors in src/pcfx/pcfx.cpp) and adds the src/control/ sources to the build.
With RRDC off, none of those sources are compiled and the emulator behaves
exactly as upstream.

At runtime the control server is itself opt-in: it only listens when the
MEDNAFEN_CONTROLPORT environment variable is set. An --enable-rrdc build with
MEDNAFEN_CONTROLPORT unset is a no-op.


 Build files
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Only autotools *inputs* are tracked (configure.ac, **/Makefile.am, m4/,
acinclude.m4). The generated files (configure, aclocal.m4, **/Makefile.in,
include/config.h.in, and the automake aux scripts) are produced by ./autogen.sh
and are intentionally not committed. Run ./autogen.sh once after cloning.
